<exercisegroup xml:id="exercisegroup-orthog-normal-plane-pts-R3">
<title>Point-normal form for a plane in <m> \R^3 </m></title>

<introduction><p>
	In each case:
	<ol>

		<li>
			Determine a <term><xref ref="definition-orthog-normal-vector" text="custom">normal vector</xref></term>
			for the plane that passes through the points <m> P </m>, <m> Q </m>, and <m> R </m>.
		</li>

		<li>
			Write the equation of the plane in <term><xref ref="definition-orthog-point-normal-form" text="custom">point-normal form</xref></term>,
			then expand and re-write in typical algebraic form.
		</li>

	</ol>
</p></introduction>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> P(-3,1,3), Q(0,-2,-1), R(1,-1,-2) </m></p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><p>
			The directed line segments <m> \abray{PQ} </m> and <m> \abray{PR} </m> are parallel to the plane through <m> P, Q, R </m>.
			Calculate the associated vectors <m> \uvec{u} </m> and <m> \uvec{v} </m> as
			<md><mrow>
				\uvec{u} \amp = \bbrac{0 - (-3), -2 - 1, -1 - 3} = (3, -3, -4) \text{,} \amp
				\uvec{v} \amp = \bbrac{1 - (-3), -1 - 1, -2 - 3} = (4, -2, -5) \text{.}
			</mrow></md>
			Compute a normal to the plane using the cross product:
			<md> \uvec{n} = \ucrossprod{u}{v} = (7, -1, 6) </md>.
		</p></li>

		<li><p>
			Point-normal form (using point <m>P</m>):
			<md> \dotprod{ (7,-1,6) }{\bbrac{ (x,y,z) - (-3,1,3) }} = 0 </md>.
			Expanded algebraic form:
			<md> 7 x - y + 6 z = -4 </md>.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p><m> P(-3,3,1), Q(4,6,-2), R(-2,0,0) </m></p></statement>

	<answer><p><ol>

		<li><p>
			The directed line segments <m> \abray{PQ} </m> and <m> \abray{PR} </m> are parallel to the plane through <m> P, Q, R </m>.
			Calculate the associated vectors <m> \uvec{u} </m> and <m> \uvec{v} </m> as
			<md><mrow>
				\uvec{u} \amp = \bbrac{ 4 - (-3), 6 - 3, -2 - 1} = (7,  3, -3) \text{,} \amp
				\uvec{v} \amp = \bbrac{-2 - (-3), 0 - 3,  0 - 1} = (1, -3, -1) \text{.}
			</mrow></md>
			The cross product
			<md> \ucrossprod{u}{v} = (-12, 4, -24) </md>
			is normal to the plane,
			but we'll scale this by a factor of <m> -1/4 </m> to <m> \uvec{n} = (3, -1, 6) </m>.
		</p></li>

		<li><p>
			Point-normal form (using point <m>P</m>):
			<md> \dotprod{ (3,-1,6) }{\bbrac{ (x,y,z) - (-3,3,1) }} = 0 </md>.
			Expanded algebraic form:
			<md> 3 x - y + 6 z = -6 </md>.
		</p></li>

	</ol></p></answer>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
